Article 59
Every victim has the right to:
a. obtain protection from family, Regional Government, police, prosecution, court, advocate, social institution or other parties either temporarily or based on a protection order from the Court;
b. obtain health services in accordance with medical needs;
c. obtain special handling related to the confidentiality of the victim;
d. obtain assistance by social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations; and
e. obtain spiritual guidance services;
f. obtain protection from threats that endanger self, life and/or property whether before, during or after the case examination process from the Police;
g. obtain health rehabilitation, social rehabilitation, repatriation and social reintegration if the person concerned experiences suffering both physically and psychologically;
h. obtain restitution for victims of criminal acts of human trafficking or their heirs in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations; and
i. other rights regulated in the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Article 49
(1) Special protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in Article 44 includes children in conflict with the law and child victims of criminal acts, constituting the obligation and responsibility of the Regional Government and society.
(2) Special protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented through:
a. treatment of children in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of children;
b. provision of special accompanying officers for children from an early stage;
c. provision of special facilities and infrastructure;
d. imposition of appropriate sanctions for the best interests of the child;
e. continuous monitoring and recording of the development of children in conflict with the law;
f. provision of guarantees to maintain relationships with parents or family; and
g. protection from disclosure of identity through mass media and to avoid labeling.
(3) Special protection for children who become victims of criminal acts as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented through:
a. rehabilitation efforts, both within institutions and outside institutions;
b. efforts to protect from disclosure of identity through mass media and to avoid labeling;
c. provision of safety guarantees for victim witnesses and expert witnesses, whether physical, mental, or social; and
d. provision of accessibility to obtain information regarding case developments.

